---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'privacy' as const;
---
<Base lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Privacy"
  description="No comment, code or diff content ever leaves your browser. Exactly what lives where, including the parts that are uncomfortable.">
<section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g18 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow acc">privacy:</span>
          <h1 style="font-size:clamp(29px,3.8vw,41px);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.031em;line-height:1.05">What you write in a review goes nowhere.</h1>
          <p class="lede">A review comment is often more sensitive than the code — it says what you think of someone's work. <strong>The extension does not collect, store on a remote server, or transmit any code, comment or diff content.</strong> This page says exactly what lives where.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">
          <span class="ct">scope</span>
          <p style="margin:0">What follows covers the <strong>browser extension</strong>, the part distributed through the stores. The <strong>server</strong> is a separate, optional component, hosted and operated by your organization: it has its own persistence, described further down.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">the extension, line by line:</span>
          <h2>What lives where</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="scroller">
          <table class="t wide">
            <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>Does it leave the browser?</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Your comments, the code, the diffs</strong></td>
                <td class="d">In the page, in the platform's own editor.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>never</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Interface language and direct shortcuts</td>
                <td class="d"><code>chrome.storage.sync</code></td>
                <td><span class="st fb"><span class="dot"></span>synced</span><span class="why">By your browser's own sync, if you have it on — not by any server of ours. Those two preferences, nothing else.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Your login, and those of the comment authors on screen</td>
                <td class="d">Tab memory, read from the page.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span><span class="why">Neither written nor sent. Gone when the tab closes.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>The displayed repository's configuration</td>
                <td class="d">Fetched from the file with your session cookies; kept in memory per tab.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span><span class="why">It's a read, on an address your session already allows — as if you opened it yourself.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Optional domains you granted</td>
                <td class="d">Held by the browser, not by the extension.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Local log of unrecognised page elements</td>
                <td class="d">The last 50, on your device: rule name and timestamp, never content.</td>
                <td><span class="st ok"><span class="dot"></span>no</span><span class="why">It's the diagnostic tool, readable from the options page.</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Usage telemetry</td>
                <td class="d">Nothing is emitted by default.</td>
                <td><span class="st fb"><span class="dot"></span>only if you allow it</span><span class="why">See below: three conditions have to hold.</span></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small measure">The extension stores <strong>no authentication token and no secret</strong>. It authenticates against no API: everything it reads outside the page goes through an address your browsing session already allows.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">telemetry:</span>
          <h2>Three conditions, not a checkbox</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">Nothing leaves until all three hold. Miss one, and the extension emits nothing.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Your organization enabled it in its browser policy</b><span class="tx">Pushed by the browser itself, out of reach of any repository. A repository cannot switch telemetry on for you.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>It declared an <code>https:</code> collection address</b><span class="tx">Its own. There is no default address, and none points anywhere near the extension's authors.</span></li>
          <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>You ticked the box, on this device</b><span class="tx">The box shows the address, and your consent covers only that one. It stays local and <strong>does not sync</strong>. Unticking it disarms open tabs immediately.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout acc">
          <span class="ct">and if all three hold</span>
          <h4>What leaves is an aggregate, never text.</h4>
          <p>The repository on screen, the configuration mode, and counters: labels used, diagnostic codes, degraded selector rules. <strong>No free text</strong> — a value that isn't of the expected shape is dropped rather than sent. The request carries neither cookie nor referrer, so the review page's address doesn't travel with it. A check measures that in a real browser on every run.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">the server:</span>
          <h2>If your organization deploys one, your organization is the controller</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">It isn't required to use the extension. When deployed, it runs on your organization's own infrastructure — never on a third-party service — and persists what it needs:</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="bul measure">
          <li>The <strong>pull request exemption log</strong>: identifier, action, who asked, timestamp, and <strong>a free-text reason if they typed one</strong> — so it may contain personal information.</li>
          <li><strong>Evaluation records</strong> per pull request, including a <strong>short excerpt</strong> — up to ~80 characters — of the root comment of each unresolved blocking thread, with its author's identifier.</li>
          <li><strong>Aggregate counters</strong> per repository and per pull request, independent of the extension's telemetry setting: they are two separate mechanisms.</li>
          <li>Current <strong>operational state</strong> needed for it to work.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout blk">
          <span class="ct">don't misread this</span>
          <h4>That list illustrates the categories. It is not exhaustive, and isn't meant to be.</h4>
          <p>This component's code evolves, and an organization that deploys it is the <strong>data controller</strong> under GDPR or its local equivalent. It — not this page — has to provide the privacy policy that applies to its instance, with its own purpose and retention. Exemption log retention is configurable; it defaults to twelve months.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">what the extension does not do:</span>
          <h2>Four things, stated in the negative</h2>
        </div>
        <ul class="bul measure">
          <li>It sends <strong>no comment, code or diff content</strong> to any third party or to its authors.</li>
          <li>It runs <strong>no remote code</strong>: its security policy forbids any script not shipped inside it, and no dependency is loaded from a CDN.</li>
          <li>It neither sells nor shares any data for advertising.</li>
          <li>It does not track your browsing outside the review pages of supported platforms.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small measure">The code is public and auditable under Apache-2.0. The permission-by-permission justification lives in the repository, and this page stands as the privacy policy for the stores.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <div class="cta-in">
        <div class="stack g8">
          <h2>None of this asks you to take our word for it.</h2>
          <p class="small" style="color:var(--fg-2)">It's all verifiable in the code, and the published bundle is checked on every release.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-btns">
          <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('install', 'en')}>Install the extension</a>
          <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('howitworks', 'en')}>How it works</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</Base>
